To retrieve the username from a JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can use the `JwtSecurityToken` class and the `Claims` property. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using System.Security.Claims;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;

public static string GetUsernameFromJwtToken(string jwtToken)
{
    var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
    var key = Encoding.ASCII.Parse("your_secret_key");
    var token = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(jwtToken, new TokenValidationParameters
    {
        IssuerSigningKey = key,
        ValidateIssuer = false,
        ValidateAudience = false,
        ValidateLifetime = true,
        ClockSkew = TimeSpan.Zero
    });

    var usernameClaim = token.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == "username");
    if (usernameClaim != null)
    {
        return usernameClaim.Value;
    }
    else
    {
        return null;
    }
}
```

Replace `"your_secret_key"` with your secret key used to sign the JWT token. This method takes a JWT token as input and returns the username claim value or null if it's not present.

