Here's an R code snippet that checks if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric:

```R
# Function to check if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric
can_convert_to_numeric <- function(x) {
  tryCatch({
    as.numeric(x[!is.na(x)])
    TRUE
  }, error = function(e) {
    FALSE
  })
}

# Example usage
x <- c("1", "2", "3", "NA", "5")
if (can_convert_to_numeric(x)) {
  print("All non-NA elements in x can be converted to numeric.")
} else {
  print("Not all non-NA elements in x can be converted to numeric.")
}
```

This code defines a function `can_convert_to_numeric` that takes a character vector `x` as input and returns `TRUE` if all non-NA elements in `x` can be converted to numeric, and `FALSE` otherwise. The function uses the `tryCatch` function to handle any errors that may occur during the conversion process. If an error occurs, the function returns `FALSE`. Otherwise, it returns `TRUE`.

In the example usage, we create a character vector `x` containing some numerical values and an NA value. We then call the `can_convert_to_numeric` function with `x` as input and print the result. In this case, all non-NA elements in `x` can be converted to numeric, so the function returns `TRUE`.

Here's a simple decorator for your Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` variable and calls `next()` if it's equal to 'true', or returns `false` otherwise:

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvironmentMiddleware implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: any) {
    const env = process.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (env === 'true') {
      return true;
    }

    return false;
  }
}
```

To use this middleware, you can simply import it and apply it to your controller or route:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get, UseMiddleware } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvironmentMiddleware } from './environment.middleware';

@Controller('your-controller-path')
export class YourController {
  @Get()
  @UseMiddleware(EnvironmentMiddleware)
  getData() {
    // Your logic here
  }
}
```

This way, when the request comes in, the `EnvironmentMiddleware` will be executed first, and it will check the `NODE_ENV` variable. If it's equal to 'true', the request will be passed to the next middleware or controller. Otherwise, the request will be returned with an error.

