To create a Chrome extension that injects JavaScript into existing webpages, follow these steps:

1. Create a new folder for your extension and inside it, create the following files:
   - manifest.json
   - content_script.js
   - background.js
   - popup.html (optional)
   - popup.js (optional)

2. Open `manifest.json` and add the basic information about your extension:

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Inject JavaScript Extension",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "This extension injects JavaScript into existing webpages.",
  "browser_action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_icon": "icon.png"
  },
  "permissions": ["activeTab", "tabs"]
}
```

3. Open `content_script.js` and add the code to inject the JavaScript into the active tab:

```javascript
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(
  function(request, sender, sendResponse) {
    if (request.action === 'inject') {
      chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, function(tabs) {
        chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, {file: 'inject.js'});
      });
    }
  }
);
```

4. Open `background.js` and add the code to handle the message from the content script:

```javascript
chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(
  function(request, sender, sendResponse) {
    if (request.action === 'inject') {
      sendResponse({});
    }
  }
);
```

5. Open `popup.html` and add the user interface for your extension:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Inject JavaScript Extension</title>
</head>
<body>
  <button id="inject">Inject JavaScript</button>
  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</body>

